full_text:
[
  {
    "SCIENTIFIC BACKGROUND AND RESEARCH QUESTION": "Current knowledge explains condensate formation through protein intrinsic disorder, multivalent domains, and RNA sequence motifs, but the role of RNA secondary and tertiary structural ensembles remains underexplored at the transcriptome scale. The primary objective is to determine if the structural ensemble of an RNA molecule systematically influences the recruitment of specific RNA-binding proteins into condensates and the resulting material properties. This research tests RNA structure as a distinct mechanism for phase separation at the transcriptome scale, which could refine protein-centric models and clarify mechanisms in neurodegeneration and gene regulation.",
    "METHODOLOGY AND ANALYTICAL APPROACH": "The study design involves harmonizing three public data layers onto a common genome build, extracting transcript structural descriptors, and using enrichment analyses and machine learning to test hypotheses. Key methods include computational secondary-structure prediction algorithms, Rosetta-based energetic modeling, Shannon entropy calculations, and supervised machine-learning using gradient-boosted trees and neural networks. Results will be validated against independent condensate datasets and orthogonal structural probing datasets, and machine-learning models will be benchmarked against sequence-only and motif-only baseline models.",
    "DATA SOURCES AND SYNTHESIS PLAN": "Datasets include ENCODE eCLIP, PAR-CLIP, POSTAR3, RNAct, SHAPE/DMS profiles from the RNA Mapping Database and GEO, condensate proteomic and transcriptomic atlases, and high-throughput condensate-propensity screens. These datasets will be integrated by standardizing them onto a common GENCODE-based genome build and transcript annotation before ingestion into a unified relational database with transcript- and nucleotide-level resolution. Limitations such as missing experimental RNA structural probing data and incompatible data formats will be addressed using computational secondary-structure predictions and a standardized harmonization pipeline.",
    "FEASIBILITY AND TIMELINE": "The project is structured in four phases including data harmonization, structural feature extraction, integrative statistical and machine-learning analysis, and validation and synthesis, although the proposal does not specify a specific project timeline. Collaboration is required because the project necessitates distinct expertise in RNA structural modeling, condensate biology, proteomics, and large-scale computational data harmonization that exceeds the capacity of a single laboratory.",
    "OPEN SCIENCE AND TEAM COMPOSITION": "Open science plans include depositing code and data in GitHub and Zenodo under permissive licenses, containerizing workflows, following FAIR data principles, maintaining a public methods log, implementing provenance tracking, and preprinting all findings. The team consists of two co-PI labs, invited domain experts, a postdoctoral researcher or senior graduate trainee leading data harmonization, and a second trainee focused on proteomics integration and machine learning."
  }
]

