]

abstract_text:
{
  "abstract": "The research domain focuses on biomolecular condensates and liquid-liquid phase separation, specifically examining how intrinsically disordered regions drive condensate assembly and maintain material properties. The scientific gap lies in understanding how these condensates maintain conserved material properties and physiological functions despite the high primary sequence divergence and plasticity of intrinsically disordered regions across orthologs and paralogs. To address this, the primary objective is to build a cross-species physical grammar atlas utilizing computational data-synthesis, phylogenetic comparative methods, and machine learning to test whether natural selection acts on emergent physical parameters rather than on primary sequence identity. Diverse biophysical, evolutionary, and clinical datasets will be integrated by cross-referencing ortholog annotations and computationally translating sequences into physical parameter vectors under standardized metadata schemas. This research provides a critical framework to identify and prioritize disease-associated variants that are missed by standard sequence-conservation predictors, ultimately informing the rational design of synthetic condensates."
}

