}

abstract_text:
{
  "abstract": "In the domains of cell biology and mesoscale biophysics, precise single-molecule biophysics measurements of protein behavior for copper-homeostasis and membrane trafficking complexes currently remain unintegrated with broad expression maps from spatial and single-cell atlases. It remains unknown whether the quantitative mesoscale biophysical parameters of these molecular complexes can be predicted from the transcriptomic and spatial expression profiles of the cells in which they reside. To determine if these single-molecule biophysical parameters can be quantitatively predicted from the host cell's expression context, this study utilizes a data synthesis approach consisting of biophysical parameter curation, dataset integration, and hierarchical Bayesian latent-variable modeling. Diverse datasets, including single-molecule biophysical literature, the Human Protein Atlas, Allen Brain Atlas, HuBMAP, single-nucleus RNA-seq, and MERFISH, will be integrated by projecting single-cell expression profiles into a shared embedding space to link spatial neighborhood statistics and expression levels to biophysical data. This research provides significant value by testing a foundational, unverified hypothesis that mesoscale biophysical states are dynamic properties emerging from cellular composition rather than fixed molecular constants."
}

