}

abstract_text:
{
  "abstract": "The research domain focuses on the mesoscale emergence of bacterial and archaeal organelles, specifically how discrete sets of genes assemble into functional subcellular compartments. The overall catalog of prokaryotic organelles remains incomplete because there is no systematic, quantitative, genome-scale framework to identify new compartmentalization strategies beyond manual curation and homology. The objective is to apply matrix factorization methods to public genomic co-occurrence data to extract structural and functional signatures of known and novel prokaryotic organelles. Genomic, structural, and omics datasets will be integrated by converting genomic co-occurrences into sparse feature matrices, combining them with structural fold predictions, and overlaying multi-omics data to score and rank candidates via a composite index. This approach is significant because it translates organelle discovery from a manual search into a scalable, statistically grounded synthesis applicable to various mesoscale molecular machines."
}

