}

abstract_text:
{
  "abstract": "In the domain of molecular and cellular biology, biomolecular condensates and membraneless organelles are emergent assemblies whose formation is currently emphasized through the role of protein low-complexity regions and folded interaction modules. A critical scientific gap remains because current knowledge fails to account for RNA as a multivalent polymer that reshapes interaction networks, lacking a predictive framework that links molecular-level binding features to the mesoscale behaviors, identities, and cellular routing of condensates. The primary objective is to determine whether condensate dynamics and cellular routing can be predicted more accurately using an integrated RNA-protein interaction network, employing a data synthesis approach that applies network science and machine learning algorithms. Diverse public multimodal datasets, including RNA-binding maps and condensate proteomes, will be integrated by mapping entities to stable identifiers to construct a multilayer knowledge graph where nodes represent molecular entities and edges represent binding, proximity, and partitioning. This work will significantly contribute to the field by modeling RNAs as programmable multivalent polymers rather than passive components, evaluating condensates as emergent network states governed by a combinatorial RNA-protein grammar."
}

