}

abstract_text:
[
  "In the domain of cancer systems biology, molecular and genomic features self-organize into mesoscale regulatory assemblies that influence cancer cell drug response and phenotypic heterogeneity. Current models relying on single-molecule or single-modality predictors cannot fully explain reversible drug tolerance or rapid pathway switching, leaving a scientific gap regarding how interacting features combine across scales to produce these emergent responses. The primary objective is to determine how these mesoscale assemblies affect drug sensitivity, resistance, and adaptive plasticity by employing a multimodal graph-learning framework with machine learning models to synthesize public multi-omics data. Diverse bulk and single-cell datasets will be integrated by mapping genomic coordinates to a common reference to construct a multilayer biological graph where nodes and edges represent physical, regulatory, and functional interactions. This research will significantly contribute to the field by unifying a fragmented public data ecosystem to model cancer drug tolerance as an emergent property of mesoscale organization rather than relying on isolated biomarkers."
]

