]

full_text:
[
  {
    "heading": "SCIENTIFIC BACKGROUND AND RESEARCH QUESTION",
    "text": "The research domain focuses on molecular and cellular biosciences, where understanding how mesoscale cytoskeletal interactions generate emergent cell-scale architectures remains incomplete due to isolated datasets and fragmented annotation practices. This study shifts cell anatomy analysis from isolated single-system analyses toward a unified framework to address the inability to predict emergent cell-scale architecture and ciliary state from individual molecular components. The primary objective is to determine whether shared quantitative rules link mesoscale cytoskeletal organization to emergent cellular architecture and ciliary state across different organisms and imaging modalities."
  },
  {
    "heading": "METHODOLOGY AND ANALYTICAL APPROACH",
    "text": "The study design involves a community-scale synthesis project that mathematically integrates existing multimodal public imaging datasets and biophysical simulations without generating new experimental data. Key analytical tools include deep learning for segmentation transfer, morphometric feature extraction into meshes and graphs, optimal-transport methods, graph-based embeddings, dimensionality reduction, and computational simulations. Results are validated using benchmark subsets, inter-rater comparisons, uncertainty mapping, and stratified cross-validation on held-out datasets to test for generalization."
  },
  {
    "heading": "DATA SOURCES AND SYNTHESIS PLAN",
    "text": "The project utilizes publicly available soft X-ray tomography, fluorescence, electron microscopy, and live-cell imaging datasets from repositories such as the BioImage Archive, Image Data Resource, EMPIAR, Cell Image Library, and Allen Cell Collection. These datasets are integrated by extracting standardized morphometric features and utilizing optimal-transport-based methods to compare shapes and dynamics within a common mathematical space. Variations in raw image resolution are addressed by computing features at multiple confidence levels, and potential repository-specific artifacts are mitigated via stratified cross-validation."
  },
  {
    "heading": "FEASIBILITY AND TIMELINE",
    "text": "Project milestones follow five phases that include public dataset curation, creation of interoperable segmentation workflows, definition of a common feature vocabulary, mathematical data integration, and incorporation of simulation outputs. The feasibility of harmonizing this breadth of resources relies on cross-disciplinary collaboration that combines expertise in cell biology, computational modeling, imaging science, data curation, and applied mathematics."
  },
  {
    "heading": "OPEN SCIENCE AND TEAM COMPOSITION",
    "text": "Open science protocols adhere to FAIR principles by providing public access to all curated metadata, derived features, and code via version-controlled repositories using reproducible containerized environments. The team composition comprises cell biologists, computational modelers, imaging scientists, data curators, applied mathematicians, graduate students, and postdoctoral researchers."
  }
]

