abstract_text:
{
  "abstract": "The research domain is genomic regulation, specifically studying how transposable elements function as regulatory elements and create mesoscale regulatory neighborhoods during human and mouse hematopoietic and immune differentiation. Current models emphasize intrinsic sequence affinity or local chromatin permissiveness but incompletely explain whether transposable element-derived regulatory activity arises primarily from individual insertions with favorable sequences or from collective mesoscale organization involving multiple related repeats. The objective is to test whether these element families generate emergent mesoscale regulatory properties when sequence grammars, chromatin states, and spatial clustering cross cell-type-specific thresholds, using approaches like high-resolution binding-site modeling and domain-adaptive neural networks. The data integration strategy involves synthesizing public functional genomics and single-cell datasets by linking annotated insertions to matched experimental data and constructing cell-state-specific graphs to represent spatial and regulatory relationships. This work provides a framework to understand how evolutionary genome expansion creates cellular regulatory innovation, shifting the view of transposable elements from technical artifacts to functional distributed molecular components."
}

