abstract_text:
[
  "In skeletal muscle biology, translational reserve defines the coordinated capacity of muscle cells and their local niche to manage ribosome biogenesis, protein synthesis, proteostatic stress, and tissue remodeling under mechanical or inflammatory challenges. However, a critical scientific gap remains in the lack of an integrative framework explaining why similar anabolic or mechanical cues produce adaptive growth in certain contexts but maladaptive inflammation, impaired repair, or wasting in others. The objective of this study is to operationally define translational reserve and pinpoint thresholds between adaptive and maladaptive remodeling by applying artificial intelligence, graph neural networks, and dynamical mathematical models to identify contributing cell types and low-reserve states. This approach utilizes public multi-omic and phenotypic datasets that will be integrated by defining a shared metadata schema, reprocessing raw data, mapping cross-species orthologs, applying study-aware normalization for batch effects, and structuring features into a heterogeneous graph. Ultimately, this research will yield a predictive, systems-level framework explaining how molecular and cellular modules interact to drive emergent tissue adaptation or failure, moving beyond descriptive catalogs of responsive genes."
]

