  "abstract": "The research domain of mesoscale biology focuses on the evolutionary emergence of cross-species protein-protein interaction networks between human hosts and their microbiomes. However, how these host-microbiome architectures assemble, reorganize, and evolve over deep evolutionary time and under metabolic stress remains largely unexplored due to siloed data, computational bottlenecks, and contaminant DNA noise. To address this gap, the primary objective is to utilize a purely computational three-phase pipeline encompassing metagenomic processing, canonical network modeling, and proteogenomic integration to synthesize massive multi-omic datasets and model the dynamic reconfiguration of these networks. The strategy integrates data from the UK Biobank, the Human Microbiome Project, ancient DNA archives, and protein reference databases by translating microbial genomic data into protein sequences, predicting their structural interactions with human plasma proteins via artificial intelligence, and linking these complexes to host genetic variants through proteogenomic mapping. This research will produce the first comprehensive deep-time host-microbiome interactome, thereby identifying novel disease targets and establishing standardized protocols for multi-omic integration."
}

abstract_text:
[
  "The research domain focuses on bacterial mesoscale organization, specifically the formation of biomolecular condensates and membraneless organelles via liquid-liquid phase separation. However, it remains unknown how dynamic fluctuations in gene expression and evolutionary mutations act as biophysical switches to regulate these condensates, leaving the integration of gene expression flux with structural biophysics incomplete. The primary objective is to construct an atlas of the bacterial condensome by synthesizing public omics datasets with AI-derived structural predictions and associative polymer models to computationally predict condensate formation thresholds. To accomplish this, dynamic monomer concentrations derived from transcriptomic and proteomic data will be overlaid onto static biophysical phase-separation thresholds to predict condition-specific emergent condensates. This work will link the disparate fields of evolutionary genomics and polymer biophysics, ultimately shifting the microbiological paradigm from well-mixed biochemical pathways to spatially organized mesoscale biophysics."
]

