  "abstract": "The research domain of mesoscale biology focuses on how three-dimensional chromatin architecture reorganization and protein-protein interaction network rewiring drive cancer plasticity, therapeutic resistance, and tumor immune evasion. However, it remains unknown how distributed genomic sequence variations and secondary genetic modifiers interact to disrupt these mesoscale assemblies and drive variable disease phenotypes, largely because structural genomic variations are rarely integrated with protein complex stability or immunological single-cell profiles. The primary objective is to construct an artificial intelligence-driven computational framework that connects one-dimensional genomic sequence variations to three-dimensional chromatin reorganization, protein network dynamics, and emergent tumor immune evasion phenotypes. To achieve this, public multi-omics datasets from repositories such as ENCODE and TCGA will be integrated utilizing statistical sequence-to-function models, positive-unlabeled machine learning frameworks, and graph neural networks. This computational synthesis will contribute a systems-level understanding of mesoscale biological plasticity, providing significant insights into the fundamental mechanisms by which cancers adapt to therapies."
}

abstract_text:
[
  "The research domain spans molecular biology, evolutionary genomics, and computational epigenomics, focusing on the phenomenon of transposable elements clustering to form emergent mesoscale chromatin architectures and regulatory networks. A critical scientific gap remains in understanding how isolated, species-specific transposable elements transcend local sequence constraints to collaboratively rewire chromatin topology at the mesoscale, largely because standard comparative genomics fails to align and model repetitive and divergent sequences across species. The primary objective is to determine how coordinated arrays of transposable elements nucleate mesoscale chromatin structures and dictate higher-order cellular outcomes in multi-lineage hematopoiesis through a computational synthesis utilizing domain-adaptive neural networks and multi-track segmentation algorithms. Publicly available epigenomic and transcriptomic datasets will be harmonized and integrated using these networks and algorithms to map clustered transcription factor binding sites into comprehensive enhancer domain registries. This research is expected to reframe transposable elements as collective drivers of genomic innovation and establish a novel artificial intelligence-based paradigm for cross-species epigenomic imputation."
]

